# rota: the box whose ~/.claude-pool is THE pool, when it is not this one.
#
# Copy to $CLAUDE_FAILOVER_HOME/pool-host (default ~/.config/claude-failover/
# pool-host). Machine-local, like `accounts` and `peers` - it names your
# machines, so it never belongs in the repo. Absent (the default) means: this
# box IS the pool host, read locally.
#
# WHY A FILE AND NOT JUST ROTA_POOL_HOST. The env var only reaches a shell that
# exported it. On a laptop that means editing a dotfile and reloading, and it
# still leaves every non-interactive caller - launchd, an agent's Bash tool, a
# cron - reading the laptop's own near-empty pool and answering confidently for
# the wrong machine. Which box holds the pool is a property of the MACHINE, so
# it belongs beside the machine's other state. The environment still wins, so
# `ROTA_POOL_HOST=other-box rota billing` overrides this for one run.
#
# FORMAT: one ssh destination. Blank lines and anything after a # are ignored;
# the first host wins and the rest of the file is ignored, because "the pool"
# is one place by definition (for BORROWING numbers from several boxes, that is
# `peers`, which is a different thing and can list many).
#
# `rota billing --local` ignores this entirely and reads this box, which is how
# the pool host's own copy answers when something routes to it.
#
# Example:
# always-on-box
